    "lawCode": "Amd §504, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4378\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the correction law, in relation to\nauthorizing commission attorneys to issue substitute jail designations\n\nPurpose of Bill:\n\nThis bill would amend the Correction Law to authorize attorneys\nemployed by the State Commission of Correction (SCOC or Commission) to\nissue substitute jail designations when a jail becomes unfit or unsafe\nfor the confinement of inmates.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nSection one of the bill would amend Correction Law § 504 by adding a\nnew subdivision five to permit attorney employed by the Commission to\nissue substitute jail designations.\n\nSection two of the bill provides that the bill would take effect\nimmediately.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nUnder Correction Law § 504(1), if a local jail becomes unfit or unsafe\nfor some or all of the inmates confined in the jail, the Commission\nmay grant the county's application to designate an alternate location\nwithin the county, or a jail in another county, as the \"substitute\njail.\" As drafted, section 504(1) provides that such a substitute jail\ndesignation must be filed in the county clerk's office by the \"the\nstate commission of correction.\" In the same manner, a designation may\nbe amended, modified or revoked by the Commission.  Correction Law\n41(1) provides that the \"state commission of correction\" shall consist\nof \"three persons to be appointed by the governor, by and with the\nadvice and consent of the senate.\"\n\nPrior Legislative History:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nThe Commission uses substitute jail designations to assist local\ncorrectional facilities in population management. If a substitute jail\ndesignation is in place, an overcrowded jail may send inmates to a\nless crowded jail in order to maintain authorized population levels,\nto maintain statutorily mandated inmate classifications and to provide\nunique services, sometimes medical or mental health related, in the\nevent that the local facility is unable or poorly equipped to provide\nthose services.\n\nOn any given day, approximately six hundred local inmates are subject\nto these substitute jail designations, and each designation requires a\nformal document that is issued by the Commission and filed with the\ncounty clerk of the designated county. As a result, there is a\npaperwork, which under the current statute, can only be issued by a\nCommissioner. Because Commissioners do not necessarily reside in\nAlbany, and their work frequently takes them into the field, it is a\n\n\nchallenge to grant counties' requests for these designations, and for\nthe Commission to make amendments and revocations of the designations,\nin a timely and efficient manner. By allowing attorneys employed by\nthe Commission to execute and issue these documents, the Commission's\nworkflow will become more efficient, and the needs of the county jails\nwill be better and more promptly met.\n\nBudget Implications:\n\nNone.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
